  "summary": "Emails obtained by The Maine Monitor and the ACLU of Maine reveal how Flock Safety sales reps cultivated relationships with local Maine police officers \u2014 including a York detective sergeant and officers in Freeport, Wells, and Ogunquit \u2014 who helped pitch ALPR cameras to neighboring departments and town officials, sometimes via free trials and contract-splitting workarounds. The article also surveys growing Maine pushback: South Portland and Falmouth have removed their Flock cameras, York plans to take its down pending a Nov. 3 referendum, and a state legislator plans a broad ALPR ban.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "I can split the cameras into two different contracts so that both items are less than $5,000, which could bypass the [Town Manager's] approval",
      "paragraph_idx": 37
    },
    {
      "quote": "They'll give you a free sample, some free cameras, and then you get kind of hooked on it. Once they get that, then it's contract time.",
      "paragraph_idx": 25
    },
    {
      "quote": "Flock doesn't do well when people are watching. Flock does well only when they operate in the shadows, and that, of course, is not how public safety is achieved.",
      "paragraph_idx": 14
    },
    {
      "quote": "South Portland pulled its cameras in June. Falmouth said it removed its Flock cameras at the summer's end.",
      "paragraph_idx": 42
    }
  ],
